  DEFENDING ARMY

  I stood determinedly at the head of my thousands.  No generals were 
present to lead the forces of the provincial Eagle chieftain.
  The provincial Eagle chieftain and I had both persuaded some natives to 
fight for them, but fortunately for me, my recruits were better armed.  
Further, my clever tactical maneuvers assured my side an undeniable 
advantage.

  Across the entire front, the 353rd Axemen screamed their hideous battle 
cries, whipped into a  fanatical fury by their diabolical King with promises 
of conquest.  They were arrayed against the 9th Eagles.  The 9th Eagles 
soared over the 353rd Axemen and attacked them.  The 353rd Axemen fought 
with the 9th Eagles and overpowered them with their thundering charge.
  Herlen's great horde overwhelmed the army of the provincial Eagle 
chieftain and took the field, dispersing their enemies.
  Chungking was overrun by the forces of Herlen.  The battle affected the 
quality of our forces:  the 429th Axemen was greatly buoyed by the exciting 
battle.  We now designate it a proud Veteran unit.  The 9th Eagles were 
unable to retreat and were disbanded into the general population.
  In early summer, I attempted an Oratory spell, but botched it.
  Conversing with passing adventurers, I heard a rumor about a heretofore 
unknown Petrified Forest in Peking (#205), held by the Giant, who according 
to legend possesses the Library of Science.
  With the advent of the late summer season, I marched on Nepal (#3006) with 
my formidable forces.
  The summer surrendered to fall.  I announced a call for volunteers in 
Nepal and was able to enlist the 446th Bowmen and the 447th Bowmen to fight 
for me.
  In early winter, I worked on my Tactics but was frustrated in my efforts.  
I conducted military maneuvers and the 447th Bowmen improved in quality.

  Thy humble servant,   Herlen


From: Rutger
To: Josh the gentle

  Honored Sovereign, 

  With the advent of the fall season I rode into Rajasthan.
  The fall cooled into a frigid early winter.  I moved into the Ind. Ocean 
2808.

  Thy humble servant,   Rutger


